-- title:
--   Theorem 7.1: $\mathbb{Z}((T))_{>r}$ is a principal ideal domain
-- statement:
--   **Theorem 7.1 (Harbater).** For any real number $r$ with $0 < r < 1$, the ring
--   $$\mathbb{Z}((T))_{>r} = \Big\{\, \sum_{n \gg -\infty} a_n T^n \;\Big|\; \exists\, r' > r,\
--   |a_n| (r')^{\,n} \to 0 \,\Big\}$$
--   is a principal ideal domain.
--
--   The ring is realised here as an arbitrary subring $R$ of $\mathbb{Z}((T))$ whose underlying set is
--   $\mathbb{Z}((T))_{>r}$ — such a subring exists and is unique, which is the content of the first
--   milestone — and the conclusion is that every ideal of $R$ is generated by a single element. That
--   $R$ is an integral domain is automatic, since $\mathbb{Z}((T))$ is one.
--
--   The proof in the source factors a nonzero $f$, normalized to have positive constant coefficient,
--   through the generators of the maximal ideals it lies in: those are finitely many (a complex-analytic
--   finiteness statement for the archimedean points, and divisibility of the constant term for the
--   $p$-adic ones), and after dividing them out the remaining element has constant term $1$ and no zeros
--   on the closed disc of radius $r$, hence is a unit.
-- source:
--   Peter Scholze (all results joint with Dustin Clausen), Lectures on Analytic Geometry, Lecture VII, pp. 42-44, Theorem 7.1 (attributed there to D. Harbater, Convergent arithmetic power series, Amer. J. Math. 106 (1984), 801-846). https://www.math.uni-bonn.de/people/scholze/Analytic.pdf

